682c62b4-308b-4752-a9bb-bfa0d8bbf889	filter-ops.overview	Filter Operations Overview	# Filter Operations Overview\n\nThe Filter Operations page is the central hub for managing filter cleaning cycles. Operators use this page to move filters through their cleaning lifecycle stages.\n\n## Key Concepts\n\n- **Stages**: Filters progress through stages like Wash In, Wash Out, Dry In, Dry Out, Storage In, Storage Out\n- **Blocks**: Filters are assigned to cleaning blocks/areas before operations\n- **Scanning**: Filters are identified by scanning QR codes or entering filter names\n- **Cleaning Reasons**: Each cycle requires a reason (PM, Type-C, etc.)\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Select a cleaning stage (e.g., Wash In)\n2. Select the block/cleaning area\n3. Scan or enter the filter identifier\n4. If starting a new cycle, select the cleaning reason\n5. If equipment groups are configured, select readings\n6. Complete any required checklists\n\n## Stage Flow\n\nFilters follow the pipeline defined in their assigned Cleaning Profile. The system enforces stage order and blocks advancement until checklists are completed.	filter-operations	1	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.048+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.048+05:30
822e5727-9e18-45cf-a5cf-1eedc85ad2ee	filter-ops.scanning	Filter Scanning & Identification	# Filter Scanning & Identification\n\nFilters can be identified in multiple ways during operations:\n\n## QR Code Scanning\n- Use the device camera to scan the QR code on the filter\n- QR codes are generated from the Entity > QR Code tab\n- Scanning automatically looks up the filter by its identifier\n\n## Manual Entry\n- Type the filter name (e.g., HEPA-A-001)\n- The system performs a case-insensitive name match\n\n## Identifier Lookup\n- Filters with registered identifiers (QR, RFID, NFC) are resolved automatically\n- The lookup checks the asset identifiers table first, then falls back to name matching	filter-operations	2	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.059+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.059+05:30
ba4b50f4-cb5a-4138-b6ac-8d77991f1fdb	filter-ops.equipment	Equipment Groups & Instrument Readings	# Equipment Groups & Instrument Readings\n\nEquipment groups link measuring instruments to cleaning blocks for recording pressure and temperature readings during filter operations.\n\n## Setup\n1. Go to Configuration > Equipment Groups\n2. Select a Block\n3. Create a group with 3 instruments:\n   - Compressed Air Pressure (Wash In stage)\n   - RO Water Pressure (Wash In stage)\n   - Dryer Temperature (Dry In stage)\n\n## Recording Readings\n- During Wash In: Select equipment group, then choose pressure values from dropdowns\n- During Dry In: Record dryer temperature reading\n- Dropdown values are generated from operating range and least count\n\n## Instrument Configuration\n- **Instrument Range**: Full measurement range of the instrument\n- **Operating Range**: Acceptable operating values (subset of instrument range)\n- **Least Count**: Smallest measurable increment (determines dropdown step size)\n- **UOM**: Unit of measurement (bar, °C, etc.)	filter-operations	3	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.062+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.062+05:30
99d11d94-d6d9-4d0f-a552-5530f9026838	cleaning-cycles.overview	Cleaning Cycle History	# Cleaning Cycle History\n\nThe Cleaning Cycles page shows a complete history of all filter cleaning operations.\n\n## Cycle Information\n- **Cycle Code**: Unique identifier (format: CC-ORG-FILTER-SEQ-DATE)\n- **Status**: In Progress, Completed, or Terminated\n- **Progress**: Visual indicators showing completed stages\n- **Duration**: Total time from start to completion\n\n## Filtering\nFilter cycles by status using the tabs: All, In Progress, Completed, Terminated.\n\n## Expanded View\nClick any cycle to expand and see the stage-by-stage timeline with:\n- Stage transitions with timestamps\n- Performer names\n- Remarks and justifications\n- Instrument readings\n\n## Cycle Detail\nClick "View" to see the full timeline with all events, checklists, and deviations.	cleaning-cycles	1	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.063+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.063+05:30
5336e367-8384-4ba0-aaf2-987759cb93bf	cleaning-cycles.timeline	Cycle Timeline & Events	# Cycle Timeline & Events\n\nThe cycle detail page shows every event that occurred during a cleaning cycle.\n\n## Event Types\n- **CYCLE_STARTED**: Cycle initiated with cleaning reason\n- **STATE_TRANSITION**: Filter moved to next stage\n- **CHECKLIST_COMPLETED**: Required checklist answered\n- **PARAMETER_CAPTURE**: Instrument readings recorded\n- **BYPASS_DEVIATION**: Stage was bypassed (deviation logged)\n- **CYCLE_COMPLETED**: All stages finished successfully\n\n## Instrument Readings\nReadings are displayed with instrument description, value, and unit. These are recorded during Wash In (pressure) and Dry In (temperature) stages.\n\n## Deviations\nIf a stage is bypassed, the deviation justification is displayed with a red indicator.	cleaning-cycles	2	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.065+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.065+05:30
c3ac24d5-b2b2-45d7-898b-801a5565506f	cleaning-profiles.overview	Cleaning Profiles Overview	# Cleaning Profiles Overview\n\nCleaning Profiles define the pipeline of stages and checklists that filters must follow during cleaning operations.\n\n## Profile Structure\nA cleaning profile is a directed graph of nodes:\n- **START**: Entry point (required)\n- **END**: Exit point (required)\n- **STAGE**: Cleaning stage (Wash In, Wash Out, Dry In, etc.)\n- **CHECKLIST**: Required survey/checklist between stages\n\n## Flow Modes\n- **STRICT**: Stages must be followed in exact order\n- **BYPASS_ENABLED**: Operators can skip stages with justification\n\n## Versioning\nProfiles are versioned. When updated, a new version is created and all assigned filters are migrated to the new version. Old versions are archived.\n\n## Profile Examples\n- **Full Cycle**: All stages with checklists at every transition\n- **Quick Wash**: Wash only with post-wash checklist\n- **Wash and Dry**: No storage phase\n- **Dry and Store**: For pre-washed filters	cleaning-profiles	1	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.067+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.067+05:30
1ad0ece6-3a95-425b-8957-7a394f6749d5	cleaning-profiles.editor	Pipeline Visual Editor	# Pipeline Visual Editor\n\nThe visual editor lets you design cleaning pipelines by placing and connecting nodes on a canvas.\n\n## Adding Nodes\n1. Click the node type button in the toolbar (Stage, Checklist, etc.)\n2. The node appears on the canvas\n3. Configure the node in the properties panel\n\n## Connecting Nodes\n1. Drag from an output port (right side) to an input port (left side)\n2. Connections define the flow order\n\n## Node Configuration\n- **STAGE nodes**: Select the stage key (WASH_IN, DRY_OUT, etc.)\n- **CHECKLIST nodes**: Select which checklist profile to use\n\n## Validation Rules\n- Must have exactly 1 START and 1 END node\n- START must have at least 1 outgoing connection\n- END must have at least 1 incoming connection\n- All STAGE nodes need a stage key assigned\n- All CHECKLIST nodes need a checklist profile assigned\n- No disconnected nodes allowed	cleaning-profiles	2	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.069+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.069+05:30
23d31224-6f1b-47f9-b689-8c1020779562	checklist-profiles.overview	Checklist Profiles Guide	# Checklist Profiles Guide\n\nChecklist Profiles are reusable survey templates attached to cleaning pipeline nodes. When a filter reaches a CHECKLIST node, the operator must answer all required questions before advancing.\n\n## Question Types\n- **YES/NO**: Simple yes or no answer\n- **YES/NO/NA**: Yes, No, or Not Applicable\n- **PASS/FAIL**: Pass or fail assessment\n- **TEXT**: Free-form text input\n- **NUMERIC**: Number with min/max validation and unit\n- **DROPDOWN**: Select from predefined options\n- **MULTI_SELECT**: Select multiple options\n\n## Creating a Checklist\n1. Go to Checklists page\n2. Click "Create Checklist Profile"\n3. Add questions with type, section, and validation\n4. Reorder questions as needed\n\n## Sections\nGroup related questions under sections for better organization. Section headers appear automatically in the checklist dialog.\n\n## Attaching to Pipeline\nIn the Cleaning Profile editor, add a CHECKLIST node and select this profile from the dropdown.	checklist-profiles	1	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.07+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.07+05:30
8c58b95d-bd37-490f-89c6-170f420e94f8	filter-profiles.overview	Filter Profile Assignment	# Filter Profile Assignment\n\nFilter Profiles link individual filter entities to their cleaning profile, determining which pipeline they follow during operations.\n\n## Assigning a Profile\n1. Go to Filter Profiles page\n2. Select a filter entity\n3. Choose a cleaning profile to assign\n4. The filter will follow that profile's pipeline during cleaning cycles\n\n## Bulk Assignment\nFrom the Cleaning Profiles page, use "Assign Assets" to assign the profile to multiple filters at once.\n\n## Profile Changes\nWhen a cleaning profile is updated (new version), all filters assigned to it are automatically migrated to the new version.	filter-profiles	1	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.071+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.071+05:30
5f725bb4-db5a-483c-894d-5a89796e8b3c	equipment-groups.overview	Equipment Groups Configuration	# Equipment Groups Configuration\n\nEquipment Groups define the instruments used during filter cleaning operations. Each group contains 3 mandatory instruments.\n\n## Instruments\n1. **Compressed Air Pressure** (Wash In stage) — measures air pressure in bar\n2. **RO Water Pressure** (Wash In stage) — measures water pressure in bar\n3. **Dryer Temperature** (Dry In stage) — measures dryer temperature in °C\n\n## Configuration Fields\n- **S.No**: Serial number of the instrument\n- **Instrument ID**: Unique identifier code\n- **UOM**: Unit of measurement\n- **Instrument Range**: Full measurement range (min-max)\n- **Operating Range**: Acceptable operating values\n- **Least Count**: Smallest measurable increment\n\n## Block Assignment\nEach equipment group belongs to a Block (cleaning area). Select the block first, then create/manage groups for that block.	equipment-groups	1	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.073+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.073+05:30
b9bc008d-21e1-4d79-8a56-52fc36534fe2	pm-schedules.overview	PM Schedule Management	# PM Schedule Management\n\nPreventive Maintenance (PM) Schedules automate the scheduling of filter cleaning based on time intervals.\n\n## Creating a Schedule\n1. Navigate to PM Schedules\n2. Select an entity (filter or equipment)\n3. Set the interval in days\n4. Define the maintenance window\n\n## Schedule Execution\n- When a PM is due, it appears in the upcoming executions list\n- Completing a PM execution records the event and schedules the next occurrence\n- Overdue PMs are highlighted for attention\n\n## PM vs Manual Cleaning\n- PM cleaning uses the reason code "PM"\n- Manual cleaning uses other reason codes (Type-C, On Request, etc.)\n- Both follow the same cleaning profile pipeline	pm-schedules	1	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.074+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.074+05:30
8085a919-890a-4a6c-b158-302b4f800937	filter-lifecycle.overview	Filter Lifecycle States	# Filter Lifecycle States\n\nFilter lifecycle states track the current condition and location of each filter in the system.\n\n## Default States\n- **New**: Filter not yet used\n- **Installed**: Filter installed in AHU\n- **Ready for Use**: Filter cleaned and ready\n- **In Use**: Filter actively in use\n- **Wash In/Out**: Filter in washing process\n- **Dry In/Out**: Filter in drying process\n- **Storage In/Out**: Filter in storage\n- **Retired**: Filter end of life\n- **Damaged**: Filter damaged\n\n## State Transitions\nState transitions are controlled by the cleaning profile pipeline. The system enforces valid transitions and blocks invalid ones.\n\n## Configuration\nCustomize lifecycle states in Configuration > Filter Lifecycle States. Add, edit, or disable states as needed.	filter-lifecycle	1	1	t	2026-03-28 18:07:50.076+05:30	2026-03-28 18:07:50.076+05:30
\.
